1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,306
Welcome back to Code Quest.

2
00:00:02,326 --> 00:00:03,690
This is Chapter 2.

3
00:00:03,733 --> 00:00:07,863
Today you will give your data names, and
make Python do the maths for you.

4
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:13,268
And an AI assistant will get the maths
wrong, while sounding completely sure.

5
00:00:13,288 --> 00:00:14,947
Your job is to catch it.

6
00:00:15,553 --> 00:00:18,658
Remember Asha's receipt from the Corner
Cafe?

7
00:00:18,678 --> 00:00:19,839
It had a problem.

8
00:00:19,859 --> 00:00:21,783
Every price was typed twice.

9
00:00:21,833 --> 00:00:23,812
Now the price of tea goes up.

10
00:00:23,832 --> 00:00:26,913
Asha must hunt down every copy and change
it.

11
00:00:26,933 --> 00:00:28,544
And if she misses one?

12
00:00:28,564 --> 00:00:31,582
The receipt is wrong, and nobody notices.

13
00:00:32,187 --> 00:00:33,965
So here is today's mission.

14
00:00:33,985 --> 00:00:37,827
A cafe bill where every price is stored
once, with a name.

15
00:00:37,867 --> 00:00:42,217
Python works out the totals, the discount
and the amount to pay.

16
00:00:42,267 --> 00:00:46,277
Change one number at the top, and the
whole bill updates by itself.

17
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:47,760
That is the mission.

18
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:50,055
Let's build it, piece by piece.

19
00:00:50,650 --> 00:00:51,990
Here is the plan.

20
00:00:52,010 --> 00:00:53,910
Five goals, one mission.

21
00:00:53,933 --> 00:00:55,973
Write variables with clear names.

22
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,460
Know the four basic types, and check them
with type.

23
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:01,950
Calculate with Python's operators.

24
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,800
Predict what a program prints, then check
it.

25
00:01:04,833 --> 00:01:07,546
And fix the errors that every beginner
meets.

26
00:01:08,117 --> 00:01:09,687
The fix is a variable.

27
00:01:09,707 --> 00:01:12,867
A variable is a name that refers to a
value.

28
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:17,903
Asha stores the tea price once, under the
name tea price, and uses that name

29
00:01:17,923 --> 00:01:18,630
everywhere.

30
00:01:18,667 --> 00:01:22,597
Change the value in one place, and every
calculation follows.

31
00:01:22,633 --> 00:01:24,862
Every app you use works like this.

32
00:01:24,882 --> 00:01:28,038
Your basket, your game score, your bank
balance.

33
00:01:28,058 --> 00:01:30,154
Each one is a value with a name.

34
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:31,749
First, values.

35
00:01:31,769 --> 00:01:36,264
A value is one piece of data, like 3, or
3.5, or the word tea.

36
00:01:36,284 --> 00:01:38,230
And every value has a type.

37
00:01:38,267 --> 00:01:39,803
An int is a whole number.

38
00:01:39,823 --> 00:01:41,857
Use it for counts, like two cups.

39
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:44,075
A float has a decimal point.

40
00:01:44,095 --> 00:01:46,270
Use it for prices, like 3.5.

41
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:50,004
A string is text, and it always sits
inside quotation marks.

42
00:01:50,024 --> 00:01:53,480
Even a digit becomes text when it is in
quotation marks.

43
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:57,212
And a bool is a yes or no fact: True or
False.

44
00:01:57,232 --> 00:01:58,591
Is Asha a member?

45
00:01:58,611 --> 00:01:58,997
True.

46
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:00,365
Now, names.

47
00:02:00,385 --> 00:02:03,008
Assignment connects a name to a value.

48
00:02:03,028 --> 00:02:07,460
The name goes on the left, then the equals
sign, then the value.

49
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:09,806
Python works out the right side first.

50
00:02:09,826 --> 00:02:12,990
Then it connects the name on the left to
that value.

51
00:02:13,033 --> 00:02:16,509
So read the equals sign as: now refers to.

52
00:02:16,529 --> 00:02:18,672
Tea price now refers to 3.

53
00:02:19,287 --> 00:02:21,531
Think of a variable as a name label.

54
00:02:21,551 --> 00:02:26,437
You stick it on a value, and you can move
it to a new value whenever you like.

55
00:02:26,467 --> 00:02:29,247
The comparison stops working at one point.

56
00:02:29,267 --> 00:02:31,847
Two labels can point to the same value.

57
00:02:31,867 --> 00:02:34,383
And moving one label does not move the
other.

58
00:02:34,987 --> 00:02:35,875
Quick check.

59
00:02:35,895 --> 00:02:39,205
Here is the digit 3, inside quotation
marks.

60
00:02:39,225 --> 00:02:40,492
What is its type?

61
00:02:51,847 --> 00:02:51,985
C.

62
00:02:52,005 --> 00:02:52,698
A string.

63
00:02:52,718 --> 00:02:55,311
The quotation marks make it text.

64
00:02:55,331 --> 00:02:57,054
Keep this one in mind.

65
00:02:57,074 --> 00:02:59,351
It causes a sneaky bug later.

66
00:02:59,953 --> 00:03:01,014
Take a breather.

67
00:03:01,034 --> 00:03:02,973
Here is what you know so far.

68
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,999
Next, Asha creates her first variables.

69
00:03:10,750 --> 00:03:11,295
Step 1.

70
00:03:11,315 --> 00:03:13,474
Asha creates two variables.

71
00:03:13,494 --> 00:03:16,137
Customer refers to the text Asha.

72
00:03:16,157 --> 00:03:18,800
Tea price refers to the number 3.

73
00:03:18,833 --> 00:03:20,517
Line 4 prints customer.

74
00:03:20,537 --> 00:03:24,443
Look closely: there are no quotation marks
around it.

75
00:03:24,467 --> 00:03:30,135
Without quotation marks, Python treats a
word as a name, and prints the value it

76
00:03:30,155 --> 00:03:30,846
refers to.

77
00:03:30,866 --> 00:03:33,264
And in Python, a star means times.

78
00:03:44,647 --> 00:03:48,557
Line 4 prints Asha, the value, not the
word customer.

79
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:53,777
And line 6 multiplies the tea price by 2,
so two teas cost 6.

80
00:03:54,387 --> 00:03:56,617
Good names make code easy to read.

81
00:03:56,637 --> 00:04:01,977
Python has rules for names, and its style
guide, called pep eight, adds one more.

82
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,095
One, from the style guide.

83
00:04:04,115 --> 00:04:07,430
Lowercase words, joined with underscores.

84
00:04:07,450 --> 00:04:10,440
Tea price, not a short code like t p.

85
00:04:10,467 --> 00:04:12,328
Two, a rule of Python itself.

86
00:04:12,348 --> 00:04:17,647
Letters, digits and underscores are fine,
but a name can never start with a digit.

87
00:04:17,667 --> 00:04:18,067
Three.

88
00:04:18,087 --> 00:04:19,677
Capital letters matter.

89
00:04:19,697 --> 00:04:24,717
Price with a small p and Price with a
capital P are two different names.

90
00:04:24,767 --> 00:04:25,117
Four.

91
00:04:25,137 --> 00:04:30,369
Do not use the words Python keeps for
itself, like True, None or class.

92
00:04:30,950 --> 00:04:31,407
Step 2.

93
00:04:31,427 --> 00:04:34,818
The type function tells you the type of
any value.

94
00:04:34,838 --> 00:04:37,410
Asha stores four values from her cafe.

95
00:04:37,433 --> 00:04:41,217
Python picks each type from the way you
wrote the value.

96
00:04:41,237 --> 00:04:43,255
A decimal point makes a float.

97
00:04:43,275 --> 00:04:45,293
Quotation marks make a string.

98
00:04:45,333 --> 00:04:49,300
Lines 6 to 9 print each value, followed by
its type.

99
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:03,940
Float, because 3.5 has a decimal point.

100
00:05:03,967 --> 00:05:05,381
And True is a bool.

101
00:05:05,401 --> 00:05:09,456
It needs a capital T, or Python will not
recognise it.

102
00:05:10,050 --> 00:05:10,525
Step 3.

103
00:05:10,545 --> 00:05:13,352
An operator is a symbol that calculates.

104
00:05:13,372 --> 00:05:16,249
Plus, minus and times work as you expect.

105
00:05:16,269 --> 00:05:18,440
Four others need a closer look.

106
00:05:18,467 --> 00:05:22,741
A single slash divides, and gives the full
answer, with decimals.

107
00:05:22,761 --> 00:05:24,657
The result is always a float.

108
00:05:24,700 --> 00:05:26,903
A double slash is floor division.

109
00:05:26,923 --> 00:05:29,800
It keeps only the whole part of the
answer.

110
00:05:29,833 --> 00:05:34,293
The percent sign gives the remainder: what
is left over after sharing.

111
00:05:34,333 --> 00:05:36,926
And two stars raise a number to a power.

112
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:40,818
Asha shares 17 biscuits between 5 friends.

113
00:05:40,838 --> 00:05:44,530
Add, subtract and multiply are
straightforward.

114
00:05:44,567 --> 00:05:47,778
Now use what you learned about the other
operators.

115
00:05:59,147 --> 00:06:02,517
Divide gives 3.4, the full answer, as a
float.

116
00:06:02,567 --> 00:06:04,198
Floor divide gives 3.

117
00:06:04,218 --> 00:06:07,187
Each friend gets three whole biscuits.

118
00:06:07,233 --> 00:06:08,836
And the remainder is 2.

119
00:06:08,856 --> 00:06:12,081
Two biscuits are left over, probably for
Asha.

120
00:06:12,683 --> 00:06:13,246
Step 4.

121
00:06:13,266 --> 00:06:15,328
Asha runs a loyalty game.

122
00:06:15,348 --> 00:06:16,993
Points starts at 10.

123
00:06:17,033 --> 00:06:21,521
Line 4 looks impossible in maths: points
equals points plus 5.

124
00:06:21,541 --> 00:06:23,121
But remember the rule.

125
00:06:23,141 --> 00:06:25,883
Python works out the right side first.

126
00:06:25,933 --> 00:06:31,183
So it takes the current value, 10, adds 5,
and moves the name to 15.

127
00:06:31,233 --> 00:06:34,460
Now line 6 doubles it, using the same
rule.

128
00:06:45,813 --> 00:06:47,475
After the bonus, 30.

129
00:06:47,495 --> 00:06:48,905
Fifteen, times 2.

130
00:06:49,483 --> 00:06:49,984
Step 5.

131
00:06:50,004 --> 00:06:55,483
When a line has several operators, Python
follows the same order as maths.

132
00:06:55,533 --> 00:06:56,943
Powers first.

133
00:06:56,967 --> 00:06:59,607
Then times, and every kind of division.

134
00:06:59,633 --> 00:07:01,563
Plus and minus come last.

135
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:03,552
And brackets beat everything.

136
00:07:03,572 --> 00:07:06,544
Whatever is inside them is calculated
first.

137
00:07:07,153 --> 00:07:09,594
Lines 2 and 3 use the same numbers.

138
00:07:09,614 --> 00:07:11,843
Only the brackets are different.

139
00:07:23,213 --> 00:07:28,463
Line 2 multiplies first: 3 times 4 is 12,
plus 2 is 14.

140
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:32,190
Line 3 adds first, because of the
brackets.

141
00:07:32,210 --> 00:07:33,570
5 times 4 is 20.

142
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:36,183
Line 4 divides first, so you get 8.0.

143
00:07:36,203 --> 00:07:39,630
A float, because a single slash always
gives one.

144
00:07:39,667 --> 00:07:40,574
Now line 5.

145
00:07:40,594 --> 00:07:43,946
Point 1, three times, should be point 3.

146
00:07:43,966 --> 00:07:44,705
But look!

147
00:07:44,725 --> 00:07:50,607
Computers store decimals in binary, so
some values carry a tiny error.

148
00:07:50,633 --> 00:07:53,952
The round function gives you a rounded
number.

149
00:07:53,972 --> 00:07:55,984
Here, to two decimal places.

150
00:07:56,587 --> 00:07:57,357
Quick check.

151
00:07:57,377 --> 00:07:59,990
Two plus four, in brackets, times three.

152
00:08:00,010 --> 00:08:01,175
What does it give?

153
00:08:12,547 --> 00:08:13,125
B, 18.

154
00:08:13,145 --> 00:08:16,516
The brackets go first: 6, times 3.

155
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:17,669
Breather.

156
00:08:17,689 --> 00:08:18,932
Roll your shoulders.

157
00:08:18,952 --> 00:08:21,460
You now have every piece of the mission.

158
00:08:21,500 --> 00:08:24,672
Let's put them together into Asha's cafe
bill.

159
00:08:29,417 --> 00:08:34,620
Back to the mission, with the new prices:
tea is 3.5, and a sandwich is 6.

160
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:37,797
Lines 2 to 7 store every price and fact
once.

161
00:08:37,833 --> 00:08:39,522
Lines 9 to 12 calculate.

162
00:08:39,542 --> 00:08:45,145
Each line uses the names above it: the tea
total, the bill, a 10 percent member

163
00:08:45,165 --> 00:08:47,423
discount, and the amount to pay.

164
00:08:47,467 --> 00:08:50,084
Lines 14 to 20 print the bill.

165
00:08:50,104 --> 00:08:51,227
Asha runs it.

166
00:08:51,267 --> 00:08:56,721
Notice the tea total is 7.0, a float,
because the tea price was a float.

167
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:13,228
Four lines change: the teas line, the
bill, the discount and the amount to pay.

168
00:09:13,248 --> 00:09:15,910
Every line that depends on the tea price.

169
00:09:15,933 --> 00:09:18,225
And she changed only one number.

170
00:09:18,245 --> 00:09:19,453
Mission complete!

171
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:22,200
Then the cafe owner has a new question.

172
00:09:22,220 --> 00:09:25,060
What is the average bill of three tables?

173
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:29,734
Asha decides to build it with an AI
assistant, one stage at a time.

174
00:09:30,317 --> 00:09:32,967
This chapter's AI skill is prediction.

175
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:38,570
Write your own prediction, ask the AI for
its prediction, then run the code.

176
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:41,640
The real output is the only answer that
counts.

177
00:09:41,667 --> 00:09:45,009
And the safety habits from Chapter 1 still
apply.

178
00:09:45,029 --> 00:09:49,676
No passwords, no private data, and you are
responsible for the code.

179
00:09:50,287 --> 00:09:51,370
Stage 1: plan.

180
00:09:51,390 --> 00:09:57,201
Asha describes the average in words, and
asks for the variables she needs.

181
00:09:57,221 --> 00:09:58,147
No code yet.

182
00:09:58,167 --> 00:10:02,247
The AI lists three bills as ints, and an
average as a float.

183
00:10:02,267 --> 00:10:07,099
That matches the rule you learned: a
single slash always gives a float.

184
00:10:07,720 --> 00:10:08,662
Stage 2: write.

185
00:10:08,682 --> 00:10:12,637
Asha writes the first three lines herself,
one bill per table.

186
00:10:12,657 --> 00:10:17,510
She asks the AI to check her names and the
types, without changing anything.

187
00:10:17,533 --> 00:10:18,555
The AI agrees.

188
00:10:18,575 --> 00:10:23,983
The names follow the style rule, and each
bill is an int, a whole number.

189
00:10:24,003 --> 00:10:25,768
Asha predicted the same.

190
00:10:26,387 --> 00:10:27,658
Stage 3: generate.

191
00:10:27,678 --> 00:10:33,539
Asha asks the AI to complete her program
with the average, and to say exactly what

192
00:10:33,559 --> 00:10:34,257
it prints.

193
00:10:34,300 --> 00:10:39,017
The AI writes the program, and says it
prints 24.0.

194
00:10:39,037 --> 00:10:40,410
It sounds sure.

195
00:10:40,433 --> 00:10:41,495
Look at line 5.

196
00:10:41,515 --> 00:10:44,882
You learned which part Python calculates
first.

197
00:10:56,247 --> 00:10:57,459
Asha runs it.

198
00:10:57,479 --> 00:10:57,933
52.0!

199
00:10:57,953 --> 00:11:02,579
The AI was wrong, and it sounded
completely sure.

200
00:11:03,187 --> 00:11:04,114
Stage 4: test.

201
00:11:04,134 --> 00:11:07,090
Here is a quick sense check for any
average.

202
00:11:07,110 --> 00:11:10,810
It must lie between the smallest and the
largest value.

203
00:11:10,830 --> 00:11:13,787
The largest bill is 30, so 52 is
impossible.

204
00:11:13,833 --> 00:11:18,444
She tells the AI what it predicted and
what really happened.

205
00:11:18,464 --> 00:11:22,688
Now it names the cause: Python divided
before it added.

206
00:11:22,708 --> 00:11:24,463
Brackets fix the order.

207
00:11:24,500 --> 00:11:26,407
Asha runs the fixed version.

208
00:11:26,427 --> 00:11:26,751
24.0.

209
00:11:26,771 --> 00:11:29,642
Now the prediction and the real run agree.

210
00:11:30,253 --> 00:11:31,317
Stage 5: debug.

211
00:11:31,337 --> 00:11:36,877
Asha types the program into her own file,
and Python stops with a Name Error.

212
00:11:36,897 --> 00:11:39,693
She pastes the code and the full error.

213
00:11:39,733 --> 00:11:41,882
She asks for the cause first.

214
00:11:41,902 --> 00:11:45,994
The AI points to line 6: averge, with a
missing letter.

215
00:11:46,014 --> 00:11:48,984
Python only knows the names you created.

216
00:11:49,587 --> 00:11:50,794
Stage 6: improve.

217
00:11:50,814 --> 00:11:52,093
The program works.

218
00:11:52,113 --> 00:11:55,847
Asha asks for at most two improvements to
her names.

219
00:11:55,867 --> 00:11:57,209
Suggestion 1 is good.

220
00:11:57,229 --> 00:12:00,713
Average bill tells a reader what the
average is about.

221
00:12:00,733 --> 00:12:04,607
Suggestion 2 says: use a double slash, for
a cleaner number.

222
00:12:04,633 --> 00:12:07,901
But a double slash keeps only the whole
part.

223
00:12:07,921 --> 00:12:10,020
Asha tests it with new bills.

224
00:12:10,040 --> 00:12:12,869
The double slash drops the point 3 3 3.

225
00:12:12,889 --> 00:12:14,549
The AI was wrong again.

226
00:12:15,153 --> 00:12:17,475
So Asha keeps suggestion 1 only.

227
00:12:17,495 --> 00:12:23,183
Here is the result: a clear name, brackets
around the sum, and a single slash.

228
00:12:23,233 --> 00:12:25,288
The AI helped at every stage.

229
00:12:25,308 --> 00:12:29,008
And at every stage, Asha checked its
answer herself.

230
00:12:29,028 --> 00:12:30,438
Two mistakes caught!

231
00:12:31,053 --> 00:12:31,834
Quick check.

232
00:12:31,854 --> 00:12:34,503
You and an AI predict different outputs.

233
00:12:34,523 --> 00:12:36,238
What decides who is right?

234
00:12:47,613 --> 00:12:47,781
C.

235
00:12:47,801 --> 00:12:49,004
The real run.

236
00:12:49,024 --> 00:12:49,662
Always.

237
00:12:50,253 --> 00:12:50,825
Breather.

238
00:12:50,845 --> 00:12:56,014
You watched Asha use AI at all six stages,
and catch two mistakes with what you

239
00:12:56,034 --> 00:12:56,933
learned today.

240
00:12:56,967 --> 00:13:00,825
Next, the four mistakes every beginner
makes with variables.

241
00:13:00,845 --> 00:13:03,088
You have already met the first one.

242
00:13:07,817 --> 00:13:10,648
Mistake 1 is the one Asha debugged.

243
00:13:10,668 --> 00:13:12,847
A name with a typing error.

244
00:13:12,867 --> 00:13:14,477
Line 3 printed fine.

245
00:13:14,500 --> 00:13:18,523
Line 4 spells it with an s, so Python
stops with a Name Error.

246
00:13:18,543 --> 00:13:20,610
It even suggests the right name.

247
00:13:20,633 --> 00:13:25,851
You also get this error if you use a name
before the line that creates it.

248
00:13:26,453 --> 00:13:27,173
Mistake 2.

249
00:13:27,193 --> 00:13:29,023
Remember the quick check?

250
00:13:29,043 --> 00:13:32,943
A digit in quotation marks is a string,
not a number.

251
00:13:32,967 --> 00:13:34,666
Concatenate means join.

252
00:13:34,686 --> 00:13:39,527
Python can only join text to text, so it
stops with a Type Error.

253
00:13:39,567 --> 00:13:42,436
The fix: remove the quotation marks.

254
00:13:43,053 --> 00:13:47,669
Sometimes this mistake gives no error at
all, which is worse.

255
00:13:47,689 --> 00:13:50,937
Multiplying a string by 2 repeats the
text.

256
00:13:50,957 --> 00:13:52,533
So you get 33, not 6.

257
00:13:52,567 --> 00:13:55,152
Without quotation marks, you get 6.

258
00:13:55,172 --> 00:13:58,279
If a result looks strange, check its type.

259
00:13:58,887 --> 00:13:59,736
Now you try.

260
00:13:59,756 --> 00:14:02,852
Remember the four naming rules from step
1.

261
00:14:14,213 --> 00:14:15,751
It starts with a digit.

262
00:14:15,771 --> 00:14:20,964
Python reads the 2 as the start of a
number, and gets confused by the letters

263
00:14:20,984 --> 00:14:21,573
after it.

264
00:14:21,600 --> 00:14:24,931
The fix: start with a letter, like second
place.

265
00:14:25,553 --> 00:14:26,115
One more.

266
00:14:26,135 --> 00:14:29,800
Think about the assignment diagram from
the idea section.

267
00:14:41,180 --> 00:14:44,036
The name and the value are on the wrong
sides.

268
00:14:44,056 --> 00:14:46,100
The name always goes on the left.

269
00:14:46,133 --> 00:14:50,266
Ten is a literal: a value written straight
into the code.

270
00:14:50,286 --> 00:14:53,034
A literal cannot be given a new value.

271
00:14:53,653 --> 00:14:56,523
Here are all four, by the last line of the
error.

272
00:14:56,567 --> 00:14:59,617
Not defined: a typing error in a name.

273
00:14:59,667 --> 00:15:03,257
Can only concatenate: a number stored as
text.

274
00:15:03,300 --> 00:15:07,380
Invalid decimal literal: a name that
starts with a digit.

275
00:15:07,400 --> 00:15:11,342
Cannot assign to literal: the name is on
the wrong side.

276
00:15:11,953 --> 00:15:12,731
Quick check.

277
00:15:12,751 --> 00:15:15,391
Which of these is a valid variable name?

278
00:15:26,747 --> 00:15:26,869
B.

279
00:15:26,889 --> 00:15:29,226
Second place, with an underscore.

280
00:15:29,246 --> 00:15:33,511
No digit first, no space, and not one of
Python's own words.

281
00:15:34,120 --> 00:15:34,959
Last breather.

282
00:15:34,979 --> 00:15:37,780
These four errors will not scare you any
more.

283
00:15:37,800 --> 00:15:41,221
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

284
00:15:45,950 --> 00:15:46,328
First.

285
00:15:46,348 --> 00:15:48,451
Four friends share a bill of 50.

286
00:15:48,471 --> 00:15:53,958
How could floor division and the remainder
tell you what each person pays, and what

287
00:15:53,978 --> 00:15:54,820
is left over?

288
00:16:06,180 --> 00:16:08,731
Write it and run it after the video.

289
00:16:09,353 --> 00:16:09,864
Second.

290
00:16:09,884 --> 00:16:11,227
A is 5 and b is 8.

291
00:16:11,247 --> 00:16:14,181
You write: a equals b, then b equals a.

292
00:16:14,201 --> 00:16:16,074
Why are they not swapped?

293
00:16:27,447 --> 00:16:31,992
Predict it, test it, then find a fix that
uses a third variable.

294
00:16:32,583 --> 00:16:33,553
Let's recap.

295
00:16:33,600 --> 00:16:37,030
A value is a piece of data, and every
value has a type.

296
00:16:37,067 --> 00:16:39,256
A variable is a name for a value.

297
00:16:39,276 --> 00:16:40,997
The name goes on the left.

298
00:16:41,033 --> 00:16:45,993
Python works out the right side first, so
a variable can use its own old value.

299
00:16:46,033 --> 00:16:47,816
A single slash gives a float.

300
00:16:47,836 --> 00:16:52,663
A double slash keeps the whole part, and
the percent sign gives the remainder.

301
00:16:52,700 --> 00:16:56,900
Brackets control the order, and round
tidies an inexact float.

302
00:16:56,933 --> 00:17:01,217
And with AI: predict, ask, and let the
real run decide.

303
00:17:01,820 --> 00:17:03,012
One last check.

304
00:17:03,032 --> 00:17:06,002
Tea price is 3.5, and tea count is 2.

305
00:17:06,022 --> 00:17:09,800
What type is the tea price times the tea
count?

306
00:17:21,180 --> 00:17:21,341
B.

307
00:17:21,361 --> 00:17:22,066
A float.

308
00:17:22,086 --> 00:17:28,320
One part was a float, so the result is
7.0, exactly like Asha's bill.

309
00:17:28,920 --> 00:17:30,240
Mission complete!

310
00:17:30,260 --> 00:17:33,710
You earned 100 XP and the Name Keeper
badge.

311
00:17:35,950 --> 00:17:37,521
Now it is your turn.

312
00:17:37,541 --> 00:17:41,340
The practice file has eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

313
00:17:41,367 --> 00:17:44,087
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each.

314
00:17:44,133 --> 00:17:48,713
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each,
including a savings tracker.

315
00:17:48,733 --> 00:17:51,149
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

316
00:17:51,169 --> 00:17:56,087
In the last one, you fix an AI's
temperature converter, like Asha fixed the

317
00:17:56,107 --> 00:17:56,613
average.

318
00:17:56,633 --> 00:17:58,271
Predict first, then run.

319
00:17:58,291 --> 00:18:01,240
Try each task before you open the
solution.

320
00:18:01,817 --> 00:18:04,070
Look at Asha's bill once more.

321
00:18:04,090 --> 00:18:06,192
Teas, 2, x, 3.5, equals 7.0.

322
00:18:06,212 --> 00:18:10,587
All those separate pieces and spaces look
a little clumsy.

323
00:18:10,633 --> 00:18:16,736
In Chapter 3, you will join text and build
clear messages from your variables, with

324
00:18:16,756 --> 00:18:17,473
f-strings.

325
00:18:17,500 --> 00:18:19,291
Do the warm-up tasks now.

326
00:18:19,311 --> 00:18:21,320
They take about ten minutes.

327
00:18:21,367 --> 00:18:23,105
See you in Chapter 3.
